# gemma4-26b-a4b-qat-w4g32.recipe -- Gemma 4 26B-A4B from Google's QAT release
# (gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-qat-q4_0-unquantized: bf16 weights trained to fit Q4_0), every routed expert
# at four bits on Q4_0's grid, the rest as the checkpoint ships it.
#
#   - Every routed expert's gate_up and down projection as two's-complement int4 codes (-8..7) with
#     a bf16 scale per (row, 32 of K): Q4_0's block, which the QAT weights were trained to, so the
#     round to nearest lands on the grid they were fitted for. moe_gemm_q at w4a8 group 32, against
#     the E4M3 codes of the experts' inputs (an f32 scale per 128 of K).
#   - As gemma4-26b-a4b-w4.recipe, the down projection's K (the expert width, 704) is not whole
#     128-wide activation groups: pad the experts to 768 first (zero rows of gate and up, zero
#     columns of down; the function is unchanged) and convert the padded checkpoint.
#   - The scales per 32 cost 1.1 GiB more than per 128: about 18.6 GiB in all, which leaves a 20 GB
#     card about 1 GiB for the KV cache and activations (fewer sequences or a shorter context than
#     the group-128 container serves).
#
#   rad-convert <padded gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-qat-q4_0-unquantized> \
#       --recipe data/recipes/gemma4-26b-a4b-qat-w4g32.recipe -o gemma4-26b-a4b-qat-w4g32.rad

blk.*.ffn_gate_up_exps.*.weight      rtn codes=i4 group=32 scale=bf16
blk.*.ffn_down_exps.*.weight         rtn codes=i4 group=32 scale=bf16
